  :adr/i64-overflow-guard-2026-07-14
  {:summary "Narrowed (not fully closed) the i64-wraparound gap
             documented at initial landing: exact wraparound parity would
             need every value represented as a JS BigInt end to end, a
             much larger invasive rewrite of this backend's whole numeric
             representation, not attempted (no known safe-kotoba-subset
             program needs it). Instead, every +/-/* result is checked
             against JS's own safe-integer bound (2^53-1) and throws
             :arithmetic-overflow rather than silently continuing with an
             imprecise value -- the same fail-closed posture this backend
             already takes for fuel exhaustion, division by zero, and
             capability denial."
   :portable-bound-check
   "the safe-integer bound is written as a portable numeric literal
    (9007199254740991), not js/Number.isSafeInteger -- so the check
    evaluates identically whether the emitted source runs under real cljs
    or, as this backend's own committed test suite does, under plain JVM
    Clojure"
   :verification
   {:real-nbb-execution "hand-verified before committing: a within-range
                         computation at the exact boundary (2^53-1) stays
                         silent and correct; an out-of-range computation
                         (10^16 > 2^53-1) throws :arithmetic-overflow"
    :committed-tests "3 new deftest in test/kotoba/compiler/
                      backend_cljs_test.clj"
    :full-suite "clojure -M:test: 145 tests / 2848 assertions, 0 failures"}
   :honest-remaining-limitation
   "this is NOT exact wraparound parity -- it converts a silently-wrong
    value into a loudly-detected exception, narrowing the gap rather than
    closing it. wasm32/x86_64/aarch64 and the cljs backend can still
    disagree on the VALUE for a program whose arithmetic genuinely exceeds
    2^53-1 -- but that disagreement now surfaces as an immediate,
    catchable exception instead of a silent divergence, consistent with
    safe-kotoba's fail-closed design (unknown/invalid inputs degrade
    toward LESS autonomy, never more), not a claim of true equivalence"
   :landed
